P1 = "Small island states contribute very little to the emissions that warm the planet, yet they are among the first to suffer from rising seas. This imbalance has given them a moral authority in climate negotiations that far exceeds their economic weight. By acting as a bloc, they have pressed larger countries to accept more ambitious targets and to discuss compensation for losses. Moral authority, however, has limits. Promises of funding have often arrived late or in smaller amounts than announced, and much of the money comes as loans rather than grants, adding to the debts of countries that can least afford them. Some island leaders have therefore begun to seek legal remedies, asking international courts to clarify what states owe one another. Whether such rulings will change behaviour remains uncertain, but they may give the weak a language of obligation where before they could only appeal to conscience."

P2 = "Popular accounts often picture the Silk Road as a single highway along which caravans carried silk from China to Rome. Historians now describe something far messier: a shifting web of routes, most travelled in short stages by local traders who rarely saw either end. Silk was only one of many goods, and often not the most significant. Paper-making, religious ideas and, at times, disease moved along the same paths, frequently with more lasting effect than any cargo. The very name \"Silk Road\" dates only from the nineteenth century, when a German geographer made it popular. None of this means the routes were unimportant. It means, rather, that their importance lay less in long-distance luxury trade than in the slow, repeated contact between neighbouring communities."

def p(text, q):
    return {"p": text, "q": q}

